  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Rajendra Singh vs The State Of Madhya Pradesh on 15 March, 2021 Author: Sushrut Arvind Dharmadhikari Bench: Sushrut Arvind Dharmadhikari 1 Mcrc-10830-2021 The High Court Of Madhya Pradesh Mcrc-10830-2021 (Rajendra Singh Vs The State Of Madhya Pradesh) Gwalior , Dated : 15-03-2021 Shri Sanjay Kumar Bahirani, Advocate for the applicant.",
    "facts": "This is the first application under Section 438 of the Code of Criminal Procedure filed by the applicant for grant of anticipatory bail. Applicant apprehends arrest in connection with Crime No.798/2020 registered at Police Station Dehat, District Bhind for the offences punishable under Sections 272, 273, 420 of IPC. The allegation against the applicant, in short, is that he was involved in selling adulterated milk from his dairy, namely; 'Sanju Dairy' run by the applicant and upon inspection of the dairy, adulterated milk, skinned milk powder, ghee and other material were seized. On the basis of the aforesaid, crime has been registered.",
    "arguments": "Learned counsel for the applicant submits that the applicant has been falsely implicated in the case. All the offences except offence under Section 420 of IPC are bailable in nature. Offence under the Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006 provides for penalty, for which separate case has already been registered. Learned counsel further submitted that in view of COVID-19 outbreak, detention of applicant in already congested prisons may be detrimental. Applicant is ready to cooperate in the investigation and to abide by all the terms and conditions as may be imposed by this Court. He is permanent resident of District Gwalior and there is no likelihood of absconsion or tampering with the prosecution evidence. In response, learned Public Prosecutor has opposed the anticipatory bail application and prayed for its rejection.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "Learned counsel for the applicant submits that the applicant has been falsely implicated in the case. All the offences except offence under Section 420 of IPC are bailable in nature. Offence under the Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006 provides for penalty, for which separate case has already been registered. Learned counsel further submitted that in view of COVID-19 outbreak, detention of applicant in already congested prisons may be detrimental. Applicant is ready to cooperate in the investigation and to abide by all the terms and conditions as may be imposed by this Court.",
    "respondent_arguments": "He is permanent resident of District Gwalior and there is no likelihood of absconsion or tampering with the prosecution evidence. In response, learned Public Prosecutor has opposed the anticipatory bail application and prayed for its rejection.",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": ""
  },
